@page "/about"

<PageTitle>about · shelltype</PageTitle>

<article class="about">
    <h1><span class="accent">~$</span> cat ABOUT.md</h1>

    <p>
        <strong>shelltype</strong> is a minimalist typing test for Linux commands, inspired by
        <a href="https://monkeytype.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">monkeytype</a>.
        Instead of random words you type real command lines: flags, pipes, paths, quotes and all.
        It builds muscle memory for the commands you use every day and teaches a few new ones along the way.
    </p>

    <h2>how to play</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Start typing the command shown after the prompt. The timer starts on your first key.</li>
        <li>Press <kbd>enter</kbd> to run it and move on to the next command.</li>
        <li><kbd>backspace</kbd> fixes a character, <kbd>ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>backspace</kbd> deletes a whole word.</li>
        <li><kbd>tab</kbd> or <kbd>esc</kbd> restarts the test at any time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>modes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="accent">time</span>: type as many commands as you can in 15, 30, 60 or 120 seconds.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">commands</span>: type a fixed number of commands as fast as you can.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">operations</span>: type a real task step by step, like freeing a busy port,
            opening the firewall or editing a config file safely. Short operations take 2-4 commands, long ones 8-10.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">difficulty</span>: easy sticks to short commands; hard adds quoting, regexes and long pipelines.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">categories</span>: focus on git, networking, permissions, text processing and more.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>cisco ios</h2>
    <p>
        Switch the platform from <span class="accent">linux</span> to <span class="accent">cisco ios</span> to practice
        configuring Cisco routers and switches. Every command is shown after the prompt of the mode it is typed in,
        like <code>R1#</code>, <code>R1(config)#</code> or <code>R1(config-if)#</code>, so you learn where each one belongs.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="accent">time</span> and <span class="accent">commands</span> draw from the 50 most common IOS commands.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">operations</span>: 23 real tasks, from saving the config to turning on ssh, setting up
            OSPF, DHCP, NAT or routing between VLANs.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">all 50</span>: type every one of the 50 most common commands once, in random order.</li>
        <li>The <a href="quiz">quiz</a> has a <span class="accent">cisco ios</span> mode too: it shows a command at its
            prompt and asks what it does, or shows a task and asks which command does it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>claude code</h2>
    <p>
        Switch the platform to <span class="accent">claude code</span> to learn
        <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>:
        slash commands are shown at Claude's <code>&gt;</code> prompt, the <code>claude</code> cli and its flags
        at the shell's <code>~$</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="accent">time</span> and <span class="accent">commands</span> draw from every Claude Code
            command you can type, with real examples like <code>/compact keep only the database decisions</code>.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">operations</span>: 16 everyday workflows, from freeing up context to reviewing a
            change before you push, running Claude in CI or shipping a feature end to end.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">all</span>: type every Claude Code command once, in random order.</li>
        <li>The <a href="quiz">quiz</a> has three Claude Code modes: <span class="accent">claude code</span> asks
            what a command, shortcut or concept does and which one does a task, <span class="accent">scenarios</span> gives 24 real situations to solve, and <span class="accent">type it</span> has you
            type the command from memory, with aliases like <code>/reset</code> for <code>/clear</code> accepted.</li>
        <li><a href="claude">claude</a> is a searchable cheat sheet of all 156 entries with examples, a command of the
            day and a way to mark what you know. It also has flashcards sorted into five Leitner boxes, so the cards
            you know come back less often, and it counts the days in a row you practice. Your progress can be copied
            out, imported in another browser or reset.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>c# linq</h2>
    <p>
        Switch the platform to <span class="accent">c# linq</span> to practice LINQ in C#. Every statement is a short,
        complete line typed at a <code>csharp&gt;</code> prompt, like <code>var adults = people.Where(p =&gt; p.Age &gt;= 18);</code>,
        and they all work on the same few collections: <code>numbers</code>, <code>words</code>, <code>people</code>
        and <code>orders</code>. Their full contents, every element, are shown above the test and the quiz, so you
        can work out what each query returns.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="accent">time</span> and <span class="accent">commands</span> draw from 74 statements in 12 groups:
            filtering, projection, ordering, partitioning, aggregation, elements, quantifiers, sets, grouping, joining,
            conversion and query syntax. Easy sticks to one operator, hard adds joins, grouping and query syntax.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">operations</span>: 12 small tasks where each statement builds on the one before,
            from summing the even numbers to finding your five best customers.</li>
        <li><span class="accent">all</span>: type every LINQ statement once, in random order.</li>
        <li>The <a href="quiz">quiz</a> has a <span class="accent">c# linq</span> mode: it shows a statement and asks
            what it does, or describes a result and asks which statement gives it, with results per group.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>quiz</h2>
    <p>
        The <a href="quiz">quiz</a> mixes two kinds of questions: <span class="accent">what does this do?</span>
        shows a command and asks for its meaning, and <span class="accent">fix the error</span> shows a real
        error message and asks for the command that fixes it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Answers are picked like in <span class="accent">fzf</span>: type a few letters of the answer and the
        list filters as you go. <kbd>↑</kbd><kbd>↓</kbd> or <kbd>ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>j</kbd>/<kbd>k</kbd> move the
        selection and <kbd>enter</kbd> answers. Quick answers earn a speed bonus, and right answers in a row
        build a combo of up to ×3. Pick <span class="accent">60s</span> for a time attack: answer as many as you
        can in a minute. After a quiz you can retry just the questions you missed, and quizzes that span several
        topics show how you did on each.
    </p>
    <p>
        <span class="accent">basics</span> asks about the 250 most common commands on their own: you see a name
        like <code>grep</code> or <code>sysctl</code> and pick what it is for. On a phone, the quiz shows big answer
        cards you just tap.
    </p>

    <h2>theory</h2>
    <p>
        <a href="theory">theory</a> explains the ideas behind the commands in 30 short chapters: the directory
        structure, inodes and links, permissions, processes and signals, memory, the boot process, systemd,
        logs, the shell, pipes, regular expressions, networking, DNS, firewalls, ssh and more. Each takes a few
        minutes to read, and you can mark chapters as read to track your progress.
    </p>

    <h2>stats</h2>
    <p>
        <span class="accent">wpm</span> counts only commands you typed correctly (plus the enter key), with a
        "word" being five characters. <span class="accent">raw</span> counts everything you typed.
        <span class="accent">accuracy</span> is the share of keystrokes that were right, including ones you later
        fixed. <span class="accent">consistency</span> measures how steady your speed was second to second.
    </p>
    <p>
        Results are saved in your browser only. shelltype remembers which commands trip you up so you can
        <a href="?practice=weak">practice your weak spots</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>built with</h2>
    <p>C# and Blazor WebAssembly on .NET 10. No server, no tracking: everything runs in your browser.</p>
</article>
